Snowflake Cloning — Complete Guide

Detailed Summary
What is Cloning?
Cloning is the process of creating a copy of an existing object within a Snowflake account. It is fast, simple, and — most importantly — uses a concept called Zero-Copy Cloning, making it extremely storage-efficient.

Zero-Copy Cloning Explained

When you clone a table, Snowflake does NOT physically copy the data files
Instead, it creates an identical metadata version that points to the source table's micro-partitions
No data duplication = no additional storage cost at the time of cloning
Storage costs only begin when:

New data is inserted into the clone
Data in the clone is modified



Source Table          Clone Table (at creation)
[P1][P2][P3]   ←───  Points to same micro-partitions
                      No new storage used

After inserting into clone:
Source Table          Clone Table
[P1][P2][P3]          [P1][P2][P3] (shared) + [P4][P5] (new, clone-only)

After modification, source and clone become completely independent — changes in one are NOT reflected in the other


