Syntax Reference
Basic Table Clone
sql-- Clone a table
CREATE TABLE my_table_clone
CLONE my_table;

-- Clone a schema
CREATE SCHEMA my_schema_clone
CLONE my_schema;

-- Clone a database
CREATE DATABASE my_database_clone
CLONE my_database;

Clone with Privileges (COPY GRANTS)
sql-- Clone a table and copy privileges from source
CREATE TABLE my_table_clone
CLONE my_table
COPY GRANTS;

Clone Other Objects
sql-- Clone a stage
CREATE STAGE my_stage_clone
CLONE my_stage;

-- Clone a file format
CREATE FILE FORMAT my_format_clone
CLONE my_format;

-- Clone a stream
CREATE STREAM my_stream_clone
CLONE my_stream;

-- Clone a task
CREATE TASK my_task_clone
CLONE my_task;

-- Clone a sequence
CREATE SEQUENCE my_sequence_clone
CLONE my_sequence;

-- Clone a pipe
CREATE PIPE my_pipe_clone
CLONE my_pipe;

Clone at a Point in Time (Time Travel + Cloning)
sql-- Clone using OFFSET (seconds back in time)
CREATE TABLE my_table_clone
CLONE my_table
AT (OFFSET => -60 * 5);  -- 5 minutes ago

-- Clone using TIMESTAMP
CREATE TABLE my_table_clone
CLONE my_table
AT (TIMESTAMP => '2024-06-01 10:00:00'::TIMESTAMP);

-- Clone using STATEMENT ID (inclusive)
CREATE TABLE my_table_clone
CLONE my_table
AT (STATEMENT => 'statement_id_here');

-- Clone using BEFORE (exclusive of statement changes)
CREATE TABLE my_table_clone
CLONE my_table
BEFORE (STATEMENT => 'statement_id_here');

Clone an Already Cloned Object
sql-- You can clone a clone — nearly no limits
CREATE TABLE my_table_clone_v2
CLONE my_table_clone;

Verify Clone Behavior
sql-- Query source and clone to compare
SELECT * FROM my_table;
SELECT * FROM my_table_clone;

-- Insert into clone only
INSERT INTO my_table_clone VALUES (3, 'New Row', 100);

-- Now they differ
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM my_table;        -- 2 rows
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM my_table_clone;  -- 3 rows

Cloning Permanent Table as Transient
sql-- Clone permanent table as transient (no Fail-Safe, lower cost)
CREATE TRANSIENT TABLE my_transient_clone
CLONE my_permanent_table;

-- Clone permanent table as temporary
CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE my_temp_clone
CLONE my_permanent_table;

Interview Questions & Answers

Conceptual Questions
Q1. What is Zero-Copy Cloning in Snowflake and why is it significant?

Zero-Copy Cloning is Snowflake's cloning mechanism where creating a clone is purely a metadata operation — no physical data files are copied. The clone initially just holds a pointer to the source table's micro-partitions. This means:

Cloning is almost instant regardless of table size
No additional storage cost is incurred at clone creation time
Storage costs only accumulate when the clone's data diverges from the source (new inserts or updates)

This makes it extremely cost-efficient and practical for creating dev/test environments, historical snapshots, or backup copies.


Q2. How does cloning work under the hood with micro-partitions?

When you execute a CLONE command on a table:

Snowflake creates a new metadata entry pointing to the same micro-partitions as the source
Both source and clone read from the same physical files — no duplication
When data is modified or inserted into the clone, new micro-partitions are created exclusively for the clone (e.g., P4, P5)
The clone ends up with a mix of shared micro-partitions (original) and independent micro-partitions (new)
Changes to the source after cloning are NOT reflected in the clone, and vice versa — they are completely independent objects



Q3. What is the difference between cloning a table vs cloning a database?


Cloning a table — simple, direct zero-copy clone of that single table's metadata
Cloning a database — recursive operation that clones the database AND all child objects including schemas, tables, views, pipes, stages, tasks, etc.

Each child object type has its own specific cloning behavior. For example, external tables are never cloned, internal named stages are excluded, streams are cloned but empty, and pipes are cloned in a paused state.


Q4. Does cloning contribute to storage costs?

Not initially. At the time of cloning, it is purely a metadata operation — no data is copied and no storage cost is incurred. However, storage costs begin accumulating as soon as:

New data is inserted into the clone
Existing clone data is updated

The new or modified data creates additional micro-partitions exclusive to the clone, which are then billed as normal storage.


Technical Questions
Q5. What privileges does a cloned object inherit from its source?

By default, a cloned object does not retain any granted privileges from the source object. This is a key security consideration. There are two ways to handle this:

Use COPY GRANTS in the clone command to copy existing privileges
Manually grant privileges to the clone after creation (by SYSADMIN or the object owner)

Exception: Tables have slightly different behavior — always check documentation for the exact table privilege inheritance rules.

sql-- Copy privileges from source
CREATE TABLE my_clone CLONE my_source_table COPY GRANTS;

Q6. Which objects are NEVER cloned even as part of a recursive database clone?

Two object types are never cloned under any circumstances:

External tables — completely excluded from all clone operations
Internal named stages — excluded from recursive clones

Additionally:

Table/user stages are cloned but will be empty
Streams are cloned but will be empty
Pipes referencing internal stages are not cloned
Pipes and tasks are cloned in a paused/suspended state



Q7. Can you clone a temporary or transient table as a permanent table?

No. This is a deliberate Snowflake restriction:

Temporary and transient tables can only be cloned as temporary or transient tables — they cannot be promoted to permanent
Permanent tables are more flexible — they can be cloned as permanent, transient, or temporary tables

This makes sense because temporary and transient tables have reduced data protection (no Fail-Safe or limited Time Travel), and allowing them to be cloned as permanent would create unexpected data protection gaps.


Q8. A cloned table has no load history. Why is this a problem and how would you handle it?

The COPY INTO command uses load history to prevent duplicate ingestion of the same file. Since a cloned table has no memory of what files were previously loaded into the source table, if you try to load the same files into the clone, Snowflake will not recognize them as already loaded and will ingest them again, causing duplicate records.
To handle this:

Be aware of which files were previously loaded before reloading into a clone
Use FORCE = TRUE carefully
Consider using PURGE = TRUE during source loading if the clone is for testing
Validate record counts after any data load into a clone



Q9. How do you combine Time Travel and Cloning? Give a practical use case.

You can clone an object at any point within its Time Travel retention period using the AT or BEFORE clause:

sql-- Clone table as it existed 30 minutes ago
CREATE TABLE orders_restored
CLONE orders
AT (OFFSET => -60 * 30);

Practical use case: A developer accidentally ran a DELETE statement that removed thousands of rows from a production orders table. Rather than using UNDROP (which only works for dropped objects) or restoring data row by row, you can:

Clone the table from just before the DELETE using BEFORE (STATEMENT => 'delete_statement_id')
Verify the clone has the correct data
Swap the clone in as the new production table

This approach is fast, zero-copy, and non-destructive to the current table.


Q10. Can you clone a clone? Are there limits?

Yes, you can clone an already cloned object, and Snowflake places nearly no limits on this. Each successive clone works the same way — it creates a metadata pointer to the micro-partitions of its source at that point in time.
This is useful for:

Creating multiple isolated test environments from a single source
Creating versioned snapshots of a dataset at various stages of transformation
Quickly branching development environments for different teams



Q11. What state are cloned tasks and pipes in immediately after cloning?

Both cloned tasks and pipes are in a suspended/paused state immediately after cloning. This is a safety measure — you wouldn't want a cloned task to immediately start executing or a cloned pipe to start ingesting data without explicit review and configuration. You must manually resume them after verifying their configuration is correct for the new environment.


Scenario-Based Questions
Q12. Your team wants to test a major schema migration on production data without risking the live environment. How would you use cloning?


Clone the production database into a dev/test database:

sqlCREATE DATABASE prod_clone CLONE production_db;

Since it's zero-copy, this is near-instant with no storage cost upfront
Run all migration scripts against prod_clone
Validate results, test application behavior
If successful, apply the same scripts to production
Drop the clone when done

This gives a real production-scale environment for testing at minimal cost and near-zero setup time.


Q13. A table was corrupted by a bad UPDATE 2 hours ago. Time travel retention is 1 day. How do you recover using cloning?
sql-- Step 1: Identify the bad UPDATE statement ID from query history

-- Step 2: Clone the table from just BEFORE the bad update
CREATE TABLE orders_recovered
CLONE orders
BEFORE (STATEMENT => 'bad_update_statement_id');

-- Step 3: Verify recovered data
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders_recovered;
SELECT * FROM orders_recovered LIMIT 10;

-- Step 4: Swap tables
ALTER TABLE orders RENAME TO orders_corrupted_backup;
ALTER TABLE orders_recovered RENAME TO orders;

-- Step 5: Drop backup once confirmed
DROP TABLE orders_corrupted_backup;

Q14. What is the storage cost implication of cloning a 10TB table and immediately inserting 1GB of new data into the clone?


At clone creation — zero additional storage cost (pure metadata operation pointing to source's 10TB of micro-partitions)
After inserting 1GB — only the 1GB of new micro-partitions created for the clone is billed as new storage
The original 10TB of shared micro-partitions is still only billed once (to the source table)
Total new storage cost = cost of 1GB only, not 10TB + 1GB



